BODY = '''<p>A pill organizer only helps if you can open it, read it and trust the list behind it. Three public sources cover the parts: the National Institute on Aging (NIA) on tracking medicines, the Arthritis Foundation's Ease of Use program for hands that struggle with small lids, and the tests run by Reviewed.com on how organizers hold up. This page puts them together so you can choose one.</p>
<p class="note"><strong>An organizer does not decide what you take.</strong> Ask your doctor or pharmacist before you change how or when you take any medicine. The NIA says your doctor or pharmacist can answer questions before you start a new one.</p>

<h2>Start With a Written List</h2>
<p>The NIA's advice comes before any product: write down all the medicines you take, including over-the-counter drugs, vitamins and supplements. The list should give the name of each one, the amount you take and the times. The same page says some people use meals or bedtime as reminders, others use charts, calendars or weekly pill boxes, and you can also set timers, write reminders or use a smartphone reminder app.</p>
<p>Filling the organizer from that list, and not from memory, is what makes the box worth trusting.</p>

<h2>Match the Organizer to Your Hands</h2>
<p>Lids are a common problem for weak hands. Reviewed had multiple testers open and close each organizer to judge the finger or hand strength it needs. Two of its findings are useful for weak hands:</p>
<ul>
<li>The Auvon weekly organizer it picked for daily use has push-button lids that stay open once opened, large high-contrast labels and one compartment per day.</li>
<li>The Sukuos AM/PM organizer has two compartments per day, with push buttons that need more force than the Auvon.</li>
</ul>
<p>The Arthritis Foundation's Ease of Use program lists Ezy Dose push-button organizers. Its page for the Ezy Dose Medtime says the push buttons and rounded bottoms make it easy to open and the medication easy to retrieve. The program describes its products as independently tested and evaluated by people with arthritis.</p>

<h2>Match It to Your Doses</h2>
<table class="art-table"><thead><tr><th>If you take</th><th>Look for</th><th>Example from the sources</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>One dose a day</td><td>A weekly box with one compartment per day</td><td>Auvon weekly (Reviewed)</td></tr><tr><td>Morning and evening</td><td>Two compartments per day</td><td>Sukuos AM/PM (Reviewed)</td></tr><tr><td>Four times a day</td><td>Weekly compartments four times a day</td><td>Ezy Dose Medtime (Arthritis Foundation), Auvon 4X-a-day (Reviewed)</td></tr><tr><td>A month at a time</td><td>A monthly case with weekly rows</td><td>Auvon monthly (Reviewed)</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>The Arthritis Foundation lists each Ezy Dose Medtime compartment as holding up to 30 aspirin-sized pills. If you take large pills or several supplements at once, check the compartment size, not just the number of compartments.</p>

<h2>Check the Lids and the Case</h2>
<p>Reviewed drop-tested each organizer, first on carpet and then on concrete. It reported that some compartments opened when dropped and that the hinges on several models may not wear well. Its double-locking travel and monthly cases were credited with preventing accidental spills, though the Auvon monthly case could still open when dropped on a hard surface. It also noted that the bottoms of the Auvon and Sukuos weekly boxes have no slip resistance, so set them on a mat or a non-slip surface. If you carry an organizer, a double lock counts for more than a good-looking case.</p>

<h2>Ask the Pharmacist</h2>
<p>The NIA suggests filling all your prescriptions at one pharmacy so your records are in one place, which helps the pharmacist spot a new drug that may cause a problem with something else you take. If you cannot use one pharmacy, it says to share your list of medicines and supplements with the pharmacist at each location. It also suggests asking whether the pharmacy has a patient profile you can fill out.</p>

<h2>Pill Organizer Questions</h2>
<h3>Weekly or monthly?</h3>
<p>Reviewed lists weekly or monthly format, travel, doses per day and drop risk among the factors to weigh. In its tests the two monthly cases it listed, the Auvon and the TookMag, had travel cases that were hard to open, so check that you can open the case before you rely on a monthly one.</p>
<h3>What if I forget to take a dose?</h3>
<p>The NIA suggests tying doses to meals or bedtime, and using timers, written reminders or a smartphone app. Ask your pharmacist what to do after a missed dose.</p>
<h3>Can the organizer replace my medicine list?</h3>
<p>No. The NIA's advice is to keep the written list and share it with your pharmacist. The organizer only holds the pills for the week.</p>
<h3>Where can I read more?</h3>
<p>The NIA's "Taking Medicines Safely as You Age" page covers writing a medicine list, the questions to ask your pharmacist and ways to remember doses.</p>'''

